qs_goal_teach() {
  case "$1" in
    'where')
      q_say 'pwd stands for "print working directory". A directory is a folder, and the working directory is the one you are in right now. The answer is a path: the folders from the top of the computer down to you, each separated by a /.'
      ;;
    'look')
      q_say 'ls lists what'\''s in the folder you'\''re in: folders (places you can go, shown in blue) and files (things you can read).'
      ;;
    'welcome')
      q_say 'cat prints a file'\''s text on the screen. Give it the file'\''s name: cat welcome.txt. Tip: type just "cat we" and press Tab.'
      ;;
    'cottage')
      q_say 'cd moves you into a folder ("change directory"). Watch the prompt: it changes to show where you are now.'
      ;;
    'diary')
      q_say 'A folder can hold more folders. Go into the bedroom with cd bedroom, look with ls, and read with cat.'
      ;;
    'compass')
      q_say 'The diary says the compass is in the drawer by the bed. The drawer is a folder inside the bedroom.'
      ;;
    'matches')
      q_say 'Two dots, .., mean "the folder I'\''m inside of": one step back up. Join steps with /: from the drawer, ../.. goes up two folders to the cottage, and ../../kitchen/cupboard goes up two and then down into the kitchen'\''s cupboard, all in one command.'
      ;;
    'pearl')
      q_say '~ means your home folder, so ~/lighthouse/shore works from anywhere. On the shore, ls does not show everything: names that start with a dot are hidden. ls -a shows them.'
      ;;
    'climb')
      q_say 'You can go through several folders in one go: cd ../tower/stairs/landing/lantern-room. That'\''s a lot to type, so type a few letters of each name and press Tab: the terminal fills in the rest.'
      ;;
    'history')
      q_say 'history lists every command you have typed, numbered. The up arrow brings back the last one, so you never type the same thing twice.'
      ;;
    'lamp')
      q_say 'The lamp is a file here. Read it: with the matches in your pocket, that'\''s all it takes.'
      ;;
  esac
}

